---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { href } from '../lib/url.js';
---
<Base title="About" description="Engineer moving from civil and structural engineering in energy into product design and design strategy.">
  <div class="page about">
    <header>
      <p class="corner"><span class="figure-num">01</span> About</p>
      <h1 class="script about__title">About</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="about__grid">
      <div class="prose about__body">
        <p class="lede lede--small">
          I am an engineer, now studying design engineering at Harvard, after almost four
          years working across renewable energy and new-build nuclear.
        </p>

        <p>
          I studied Civil, Environmental and Geomatic Engineering at UCL and joined Mott
          MacDonald straight afterwards, working first in Seoul and then in London. The
          first couple of years were in renewable energy, on offshore wind and solar. After
          that I moved onto new-build nuclear, where most of my time went on structural
          analysis and on getting engineering, safety and environmental evidence into a
          form that stakeholders could actually make decisions from.
        </p>

        <p>
          Looking back, the parts I was best at were rarely the calculations themselves.
          They were the translations: turning a vague client requirement into something a
          team could build against, turning a technical constraint into a decision someone
          could take, and helping peer reviewers and regulators agree on what counted as
          sufficient evidence. I did not think of it as design at the time, because in
          engineering it tends to go by another name, but that is what it was.
        </p>

        <p>
          What made me want to move was what the work was optimised for. Cost and programme
          are real constraints and I am not sentimental about them, but a solution can
          satisfy both of them and still land badly on the people who have to live with it.
          Closing that gap is rarely anyone&rsquo;s job, and I would rather work somewhere
          that it is.
        </p>

        <p>
          I am now at Harvard, on the Master in Design Engineering, which started with
          preterm in early August 2026.
        </p>

        <h2>Two baselines</h2>

        <p>
          I am a dual Korean and British citizen and I grew up in both languages, which
          turns out to be most useful as a way of comparing things. Having a second
          reference point is usually how I notice that something is a problem at all,
          rather than simply the way things are done. Most of what I find worth working on
          starts as a small difference between the two places that nobody around me had
          thought to explain.
        </p>

        <p>
          It shows up in how I work with people as much as in what I notice. I have worked
          in Korean and in English on the same projects, spent two years coordinating
          between teams in Seoul and London, and chaired a committee of twenty five
          representatives across Asia and Australasia serving a network of around three
          hundred members. Most of that job was making one group legible to another, which
          is the same comparison applied to people rather than places.
        </p>

        <h2>The engineering half</h2>

        <p>
          Almost everything I have worked on so far had to stand up. Offshore wind
          substructures, solar plants, and then nuclear buildings, where a design is not
          finished when it looks right but when it has been checked against Eurocode and
          AISC and somebody independent agrees the numbers hold. I can build the model, run
          the analysis and verify it by hand, and I have worked somewhere that being wrong
          is expensive.
        </p>

        <p>
          That is the part I expect to be less common among designers, and it is why I am
          useful earlier than the point where a concept usually gets handed over. I can
          take an idea as far as whether it can actually be built, what it would take to
          put up and take apart again, and which constraint is the one that will decide it.
          I am comfortable reading a standard, and comfortable in the conversation where a
          technical constraint has to turn into a decision somebody can act on.
        </p>

        <p>
          None of that substitutes for design judgement, and being able to do the
          engineering is not the same as knowing what is worth building. That is a large
          part of why I am at Harvard. But it does mean I am not guessing when a proposal
          meets the physical world, and I do not have to give the problem away at that
          point. The same instinct is why I am learning to build rather than only specify:
          I am working through CS50 at the moment, and this site is the first thing I have
          made by hand rather than assembled with tools.
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="about__side">
        {/* Temporarily removed at Elina's request. Put back by uncommenting.
        <div class="about__card">
          <p class="eyebrow">Looking for</p>
          <p>Summer 2027 internships in product design engineering, design technology, and hardware or systems design.</p>
        </div>
        */}
        <div class="about__card">
          <p class="eyebrow">Contact</p>
          <dl class="about__contact">
            <dt>Email, personal</dt>
            <dd><a href="mailto:elinahsyj@gmail.com">elinahsyj@gmail.com</a></dd>

            <dt>Email, Harvard</dt>
            <dd><a href="mailto:elina_lee@mde.harvard.edu">elina_lee@mde.harvard.edu</a></dd>

            <dt>LinkedIn</dt>
            <dd><a href="https://linkedin.com/in/ejl25" rel="me">linkedin.com/in/ejl25</a></dd>

            <dt>GitHub</dt>
            <dd><a href="https://github.com/shuiee" rel="me">github.com/shuiee</a></dd>
          </dl>
        </div>
        <p><a href={href('/cv/')}>Full CV &rarr;</a></p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .about { padding-top: var(--space-8); }
  .about__title {
    font-size: clamp(var(--size-8), 9vw, var(--size-10));
    line-height: 0.95;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    margin: var(--space-4) 0 var(--space-7);
  }
  .about__grid { display: grid; gap: var(--space-8); }
  @media (min-width: 950px) {
    .about__grid { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 260px; gap: var(--space-10); }
  }
  .about__body h2 {
    font-size: var(--size-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-8);
    padding-top: var(--space-5);
    border-top: var(--border-hair) solid var(--rule);
  }
  .about__side { display: grid; gap: var(--space-5); align-content: start; font-size: var(--size-2); }
  .about__card { border-top: var(--border-mark) solid var(--ink); padding-top: var(--space-3); }
  .about__card p + p { margin-top: var(--space-2); }

  /* Labelled so a reader can tell at a glance which address is which. */
  .about__contact { margin: var(--space-3) 0 0; }
  .about__contact dt {
    font-size: var(--size-1);
    letter-spacing: var(--track-label);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-quiet);
  }
  .about__contact dd { margin: var(--space-1) 0 0; word-break: break-word; }
  .about__contact dd + dt { margin-top: var(--space-4); }
</style>
